A||N||8||87gt guy||NEW Rear Brake Caliper freezing??? Need Help||||1||
Z||000000||87gt guy||11-17-2001||10:13 AM||||Hey guys, I got an 87 GT. The brakes were shot when i got it so i had the rear brakes caliper and rotor changed. The brakes were put on and bled. I go to drive it up the street abd the brakes will not release. I know it isn't the e-brake, could it be the hoses? If so, which hoses? Is it true that they could push fluid out and not suck it back in when you release the pedal? I NEED HELP, I want to get this baby on the ROAD! Post what you think.||||reg||1
Z||000001||avengador1||11-17-2001||11:17 AM||||Did you make sure that the parking cable adjustment is not too tight? If it is the brakes will drag.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87gt guy||11-17-2001||11:40 AM||||yeah, the cable was adjusted fine, even used a feeler gauge to set the gap, but it like the brakes will close but not release. <P>NICE GT MAN||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||11-17-2001||02:31 PM||||OK, so are you sure that the parking brake lever is releasing? Remember that you have to press the brake lever button and lower it while pressing the button. Does the brake light in the dash go off when you release the brake? That is how I tell that mine is released. Also there should be some slack in the parking brake cable (under the car) when it is released.<p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||87gt guy||11-18-2001||09:46 AM||||i do know how to release the brake. i was asking is it possible the brake line could push out, but not suck back in.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-18-2001||04:51 PM||||Yes it's posible. You're looking at the soft line to the calipers...<P>It's usually caused by oil soaking the jacket of the lines. the oil soaks into the line and swells the inner layer. Since the MC is capable of delivering hundreds of PSI, the fluid goes out but can't get back.<P>Could also be a bad caliper... <P>Does it let go when the bleeder is opened? <P>Yes, lines or MC problem. If it's only one caliper, it's probly the line.<BR>No, bad caliper or hung hand brake parts.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||87gt guy||11-20-2001||09:13 PM||||GRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRRR!!!!!!!!!!<BR>I went to change the hose, and I can't get it unbolted off the steel one. Now they are both stripped and I don't feel like messin with it, can I cut out that part of the steel line and do a straight splice? Do I need and special tools? Also the calpiers are new and they work for a little bit then start to freeze up,so i am guessing its the hose.||||reg||8
Z||000007||rogergarrison||11-20-2001||09:31 PM||||Why not just replace the line too. They do corrode and arent that expensive. If you got any brake fluid on pads or rotor it could also cause that wheel to lock up.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ka4nkf||11-22-2001||01:44 AM||||I know you said the E-brake was adjusted  but if your E-brake lever clicks less than 5 clicks when pulled then it is too tight. also go to Ogres cave he has the correct info for adjusting the rear brakes||||reg||1
